WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.558 --> 00:00:03.660
- [Narrator] This 165,000
square foot warehouse

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:04.560
is piled high

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.560
with liquidated goods, including
the never-ending stream

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.637
of things we've all been returning.

00:00:09.637 --> 00:00:10.470
(boxes clattering)

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.530
Liquidation company Quicklotz
estimates that about 30%

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.590
of the products it receives
are clearly used returns.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.150
- A little charger, watch,

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.040
this is like a very expensive Swiss watch.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.720
Of course, underwear.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.970
- [Narrator] Customer
returns are skyrocketing,

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:27.180
filling landfills and warehouses
like this one to the brim.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.790
- A pallet like this we estimate to be

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.670
about 20, $30,000 in retail value.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.200
- [Narrator] So how did it all get here?

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.330
And how are retailers feeling the impact

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.943
of an increasing number of unwanted goods.

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:41.730
When that pair of pants you bought online

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.310
doesn't fit quite right,
the solution is easy.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.320
Return it and get a refund.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.700
But after you mail them back,

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.460
where those pants end up
is a bit more complicated.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.820
- The best of all possible answers

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:58.820
is that the good gets repackaged
and goes back on the shelf.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.270
So it's ready for sale as a new item.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.340
- [Narrator] But getting there is costly.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.770
Reverse logistics company Optoro estimates

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.220
that on average it costs
66% of the price of an item

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.020
to process its return.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.930
For example, if those pants
you returned were $50,

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.560
it would cost the retailer
$33 to take them back.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.350
So many returns travel
through a complicated web

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.800
of warehouses, secondary
resellers, and landfills.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.960
First up is a returns processing center

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.150
where the item gets inspected for damage.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.400
Every company has a
different set of standards

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.904
for what can be repackaged and sold again,

00:01:34.904 --> 00:01:37.620
(cardboard clattering)

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.840
and plenty of things don't meet them.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.080
Optoro says that many
retailers throw away over 1/4

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.270
of returned goods in 2019.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.400
The amount of returns and excess inventory

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.350
that retailers send to landfills

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.840
has more than doubled since 2016

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.530
from 4 billion pounds
to 9.6 billion pounds.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:57.630
But everything else is harder to track.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.490
The rest of the unwanted goods filter

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.350
through a secondary resale market,

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.020
including retailer outlets
and online auctions.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.107
One path leads to liquidation
companies like Quicklotz.

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.920
- Retailers, their business is to retail.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.640
Their business is not to
deal with rejected inventory.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.080
They figure out

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.118
how to just streamline the
process by liquidating.

00:02:19.118 --> 00:02:20.940
(truck beeping)

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.620
- Companies like Nordstrom, Target

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.380
and Best Buy send products
by the truck full.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.180
Those trucks are filled with pallets

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.760
of both customer returns
and excess inventory

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.580
or items that never
sold in the first place.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.190
- Everything comes bundled
together as liquidation,

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.980
and a lot of times, even
merchandise that are brand new.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.180
They could have been returns

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.760
but they're hard to identify
because they got returned

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.250
with tags in brand new condition.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.110
- [Narrator] The company
sells pallets wholesale

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:49.710
but it also sorts their items
by category and condition.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.630
Its clients are other resellers,

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.000
from people listing on
marketplaces like eBay

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.340
or Poshmark to mom and pop discount stores

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.020
to flea market stands,

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.960
and now companies are dealing
with even more returns.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.240
According to the National
Retail Federation,

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.630
customer returns across the
retail industry are growing.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:10.650
As e-commerce sales explode,
online returns do too.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.990
They've more than doubled
in the past few years

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:17.990
from 9.6% in 2019 to 20.8% in 2021.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.350
- So when we shop at a
store, we see, feel, smell.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:23.910
We know what we're getting.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.858
When we shop online, we
don't have that sensation

00:03:26.858 --> 00:03:30.257
and so we end up buying more items

00:03:30.257 --> 00:03:33.411
so that we can see,
feel, smell, touch them.

00:03:33.411 --> 00:03:34.800
(taping whooshing)

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.110
- [Narrator] Retailers
themselves have made it easier

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:39.690
with long return windows
and free shipping.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:44.690
- Those policies that especially
Amazon created years ago

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.360
has increased that
convenience to the customer.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.850
And because one retailer started,

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.650
everybody has to follow suit.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.750
- [Narrator] Amling says retailers
would rather eat the cost

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:57.090
of returns than disappoint customers,

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:00.510
but at the end of the day,
returns still affect consumers.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:05.370
- Those costs go into the price
that they charge for items

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:08.648
and ultimately that cost is born by us.

00:04:08.648 --> 00:04:11.310
(forklift rumbling)

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.980
- [Narrator] For retailers
overwhelmed with returned goods,

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.340
offloading them to
liquidators can be a relief.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:18.630
- [Alan] It's a painful process

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.520
and they just want to get rid of it.

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:22.410
They just want someone else to do it.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.900
- [Narrator] And it can be
a way to recoup some loss.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:26.550
Amling says if retailers think

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:29.010
that those goods will end up
in a landfill, selling them

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:32.460
to liquidators instead can
be an appealing choice.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:35.160
Still returns eat into the bottom line.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.040
- Retailers in a high-margin
business and a large part

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.710
of that are all of the costs that go

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:44.580
in to things like returns.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.910
That is actively cutting into the margins

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:49.440
that retailers make.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.290
- [Narrator] But it's tricky
to track the actual cost.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:57.290
- The responsibility is
diffused among different groups

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.070
within the organization.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:03.510
Too many companies are
not measuring the cost

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:06.930
that they're spending
on reverse logistics.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:08.280
- [Narrator] As returns increase,

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.950
some retailers are changing their policies

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:12.510
but it's not widespread.

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:15.870
- Companies that are selling
goods that can be found

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:20.870
on other websites are gonna
find it much more difficult

00:05:20.895 --> 00:05:25.560
because if I can find the
same product somewhere else

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:28.980
and it's free returns,
that's where I'm going.

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:30.600
- [Narrator] But despite
making small shifts,

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:31.710
Amling says retailers

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:34.830
aren't prioritizing dealing
with returned items.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:38.370
- Right now, consumers have no idea

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:42.540
what happens to a return once they,

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.240
let's say drop it off at a UPS store.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:49.340
Once that becomes
transparent, they will change.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:53.321
(pensive music)
(packaging rattling)

